# Hindsight's own Go suite. Two commands, one flag apart, and the cache serves
# one and refuses the other. That contrast is the entire point of this target.
#
#   go test -count=1 ./...                      tree unchanged  -> SERVED
#   go test -count=1 -coverprofile=cover.out    writes cover.out -> REFUSED
#
# Measured: 7499 ms for the coverprofile run, so it is nowhere near the
# duration floor — the refusal is purely the purity gate. Before the command
# the tree hashes to e04056a1..., after it 34bd1c7f..., because cover.out
# lands in the worktree and this repo does not gitignore it. Nobody declared
# `-coverprofile` impure and no list of bad flags is consulted. The gate hashed
# the tree twice and the two hashes disagreed.
#
# Deleting cover.out restores the tree to e04056a1..., which is the other half
# of the claim: the gate is measuring the tree, not remembering a grudge.
#
# `go vet ./...` (223 ms) and `go build ./...` (370 ms) were both measured and
# both rejected — under the 500 ms floor with Go's build cache warm, which is
# the normal state on a developer machine.
#
# The impure command is deliberately LAST. It leaves cover.out behind, which
# changes the tree every later command would be keyed against.

#expect:served
go test -count=1 ./...
#expect:refused
go test -count=1 -coverprofile=cover.out ./...
